\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {38}{\ignorespaces \xpg@aux {}{french}$r$ bits) and C (the capacity: $c$ bits). Both R and C are initialised to 0 . In each step, an input block is mixed with the R part of the current state using an XOR function, and then the function $f$ is applied to both parts of the state to produce a random permutation of the ( $r+c$ ) bits in the state. After the last block of input has been dealt with, the actual $n$-bit hash value is derived from the $r$ bits in the R part of the final state.}}{129}{figure.0.38}\protected@file@percent }
